# define.constraint.kind=invariant

invariant = a statement about the domain, true in every scenario a reader can observe. `reserved boards ≤ stock, per slot` · `lesson bill = sum of its lines` · `each lesson reservation carries the consent its surfer requires`
never list every scenario: declare the baseline and the boundary cases in domain words; induction carries the rest. best-guess each case; itemize the unconfident ones as fulcrums — never halt to ask
why: it defines correct. without it, a defect shows only when production breaks it
one of three **constraints**: invariant (we keep it, inside) · assumption (the world keeps it) · guarantee (we keep it, for consumers at a release boundary)
two grains, never mixed: the constraint says **what** must be true, in domain words; the **enforcement** asserts it is not broken, in code, per route. a declaration never names its enforcement
enforcement kinds: **structural** — **compilation** (a type, a closed enum) or **abstraction** (no path reaches the break: a domain object that refuses construction, a boundary parse, one write path, a storage constraint or optimistic lock) · **operational** — **devtime** (a test or a play) · **runtime** (an assertion in the path; fail fast) · **monitor** (out of band, after the fact). prefer the earliest. a guard in one caller is neither: the next path skips it
ask of every change: **which constraint, and how is it enforced?**
see also: `define.constraint.grain=enforcement` (architect) — the ladder in full · `rule.forbid.scattered-invariant-guards` · `rule.forbid.check-then-write` (architect) — the two antipatterns that skip an enforcement
